        "questionText": "During the early twentieth century, Carl Panzram was an American serial killer who committed various types of crime in {1} countries. Among thousands of other crimes from theft to rape committed in many different countries, he is claimed to have killed 21 people. He was also {2} in one of the most extraordinary prison friendships on record. He became a close friend with the guard in the prison. A new book by John Borowski, Panzram at Leavenworth, provides a collection of impressive archival documents about Panzram. Carl Panzram’s life of {3}, pain, and torture is an important lesson in how corporal punishment can create a hate-filled human being. \n\nAdapted from https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/shadow-boxing/202007/the-meanest-man-who-ever-lived\n\nDREAM BIG!\n\nThe size of your dreams must always {4} your current capacity to achieve them. If you start off with a small dream, you may not have much left when it is fulfilled. Your dreams might \n{5} you, but this should not discourage you from turning them into action. Instead of being afraid of a big step, {6} it into smaller actionable steps, which are important signs of progress.\n\nIf you want to grow as a person at a personal and professional level, it is time to embrace the art of testing your limits. The real change occurs when we {7} to dream of more. You should be brave and take risks. The more you say, “I can’t do that,” the more you’ll believe this. Therefore, when you’re thinking of a new crazy idea, change your reaction towards it. Leave aside the negativity and mental limitations that keep you back and be {8} to try it. Of course, not everything will work out, but it is still important to have an optimistic {9} on life that will make you more open to new ideas.\n\nAdapted from https://medium.com/swlh/how-to-explore-your-real-potential-by-testing-your-limits-ce56af6bfcf9\n\nSIBLING RELATIONSHIP\n\nWhile many kids are lucky enough to become best friends with their siblings, it is common for brothers and sisters to fight. Often, sibling {10} starts even before the second child is born and continues as the kids grow and compete for everything from toys to attention. As kids reach different stages of development, their needs {11}.It's natural for kids' changing needs, anxieties, and identities to affect how they relate to one another. For example, toddlers are naturally protective of their toys and belongings. If a baby brother or sister picks up the toddler's toy, the older child may react aggressively. School-age kids often have a strong concept of fairness, so they might not understand why siblings of other ages are {12} differently by adults. \n\nWhile it may be common for brothers and sisters to fight, it is certainly not pleasant for anyone in the house. A family can only {13} a certain amount of conflict, so when there is a conflict between the siblings, they want to eliminate it. However, they should step in only if there is a danger of physical harm. Otherwise, they risk creating other problems.\n\nAdapted from  https://kidshealth.org/en/parents/sibling-rivalry.html",
